fixed chassis: One Smith rack with two parallel vertical guide rails and one bar sliding on both. Paired hooks and adjustable safety stops engage the frame; plate sleeves sit outside the bearings. Same rack across exercises. A removable adjustable bench is present for presses and absent for squats.","reject":"Changed frame feet, extra stack/lever, floating component or cropped load mechanism.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"exercise-configuration","views":["adjustment","setup","start","end"],"observe":"Attachment and hardware: a Smith machine: a barbell fixed on two vertical steel guide rails with rack hooks and safety stops, the bar set at upper-chest height","reject":"Wrong attachment, resistance mechanism, cable routing or rail/pad orientation.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"adjustments","views":["adjustment","setup"],"observe":"Before movement: bar across the upper back, feet about shoulder width and slightly in front of the bar. The adjustment pin or latch is visibly engaged. Body-relative settings are not universal seat numbers or prescribed kilograms.","reject":"Unlatched adjustment, invented numbered setting/load, pad or grip contradicting the setup.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"setup-contacts","views":["setup","start","end"],"observe":"Supporting contacts and grip: bar across the upper back, feet about shoulder width and slightly in front of the bar","reject":"Missing pad/foot/hand contact, fingers through the handle or required contact hidden from inspection.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"start-pose","views":["start"],"observe":"Working phase starts: squatted down, thighs about parallel to the floor, back straight","reject":"Wrong start position, range, grip or support.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"end-pose","views":["end"],"observe":"Working phase ends: standing tall, legs straight","reject":"Wrong endpoint, unintended lockout, torso compensation or shifted fixed hardware.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"mechanical-travel","views":["start","end"],"observe":"Motion: straight up along the guide rails. Lever rotates about its actual pivot; sled/bar stays on rails; cable stays connected along the same route.","reject":"Free-floating handle, cable through body/frame, impossible lever translation or moving camera/chassis.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"critical-alignment","views":["alignment"],"observe":"Show these critical supporting contacts at the start position: bar across the upper back, feet about shoulder width and slightly in front of the bar; squatted down, thighs about parallel to the floor, back straight","reject":"Critical contact, joint or pivot obscured; roller in wrong location, incorrect alignment or pose.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"technique","views":["setup","start","end","alignment"],"observe":"Visible technique agrees with: Unhook the bar by twisting it off the hooks, then brace before the first rep.; Sit down under control (2–3 s) to at least thighs parallel, knees tracking over the toes.; Keep the whole foot planted and the chest up; the bar path is fixed, so your foot position sets the balance.; Drive up and breathe out past the hardest part; stop short of locking the knees.","reject":"Depiction encourages these authored mistakes: Training without the safety stops set.; Feet right under the bar (knees forced far forward, heels up) or far in front (the lower back rounds at the bottom).","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"same-actor","views":["setup","start","end","alignment"],"observe":"Same adult proportions and limb lengths; hands have five digits with natural occlusion, thumb opposing fingers around the grip.","reject":"Extra/fused digits or limbs, changed proportions, handle detached from hand or impossible bend.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"house-language","views":["identity","adjustment","setup","start","end","alignment"],"observe":"Ivory ground, thin brown contour, matte graphite frame, cream pads, grey hardware/person, orange only on adjustment controls; one scene.","reject":"Photorealism, glossy render, food props, lettering, logos, arrows, diagram insets or muscle overlays.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."},{"id":"framing","views":["identity","setup","start","end"],"observe":"All required contacts and working mechanism visible; start/end share camera, scale and crop; 8% outer margin.","reject":"Cropped working lever, foot/head, altered crop or contact hidden by frame.","evidence":"Record an observation and normalized bounding box marking the relevant component/contact."}],"sourceCorrections":[],"languageVersion":"machine-language-2.0","language":{"version":"machine-language-2.0","policySource":"art/image-requirements.json","policyScope":"Inherit the site''s thin warm contours, muted matte cel shading, ivory ground and no-text/no-arrow rendering lock. Fitness explicitly replaces culinary subject/hand-only framing with a whole adult mannequin and commercial gym equipment. No food props, ingredient rules, manufacturer identity or photorealism.","styleReference":"public/media/illustrations/nigiri/technique-4.webp","styleReferenceRole":"Palette and linework only; never use food geometry as machine geometry.","rendering":"Scientific educational gym illustration in the site''s hand-drawn Japanese-animation visual language. Thin even warm-brown contours, smooth matte two-tone cel shading, no gradients, grain, gloss or photographic texture. Plain warm ivory background edge to edge, one restrained ground shadow. No environment, typography, numbers, logos, arrows, diagrams, borders or insets. One scene and one camera per image.","palette":{"background":"#faf6ef","outline":"#4a3a2a","frame":"#3b3632","upholstery":"#efe6d6","hardware":"#c9c3b8","adjustmentControls":"#b83d0b","actorBase":"#d8cfc3","actorShadow":"#b9ad9e"},"actor":"One neutral adult training mannequin, consistent athletic proportions and scale in all views; smooth faceless head, warm-grey body, no sexual anatomy, hair or clothing changes. Anatomically plausible joints and five digits per hand with natural occlusion, thumb opposing fingers around the grip; no mittens, fused digits or extra limbs. Keep the same limb lengths, handedness, stance and supporting contacts across phases. No painted muscle overlays; muscle maps are a separate asset family.","machineLock":"Preserve the approved machine''s frame silhouette, frame feet, weld topology, pad dimensions, upholstery, pivot positions, rail angle, cable routing, adjustment controls and attachment family. Moving handles, lever arms, sled and cable endpoints move as one physically connected mechanism. Never move fixed pads or invent a second mechanism to accommodate a pose. Use a generic training station, not an unverified branded model.","cameraLock":"Body views use a 50mm-equivalent orthographic-like view without wide-angle distortion, camera level, same camera/scale/crop across setup, start and end; adult and full working mechanism visible with 8% outer margin. Changing camera is permitted only for the named adjustment/alignment detail views. Camera settings are prompt constraints, not OpenAI API parameters.","api":{"model":"gpt-image-2-2026-0